### What does the consumer need?

```java
package com.bank.consumer;

import org.apache.kafka.clients.consumer.ConsumerConfig;
import org.apache.kafka.clients.consumer.ConsumerRecords;
import org.apache.kafka.clients.consumer.KafkaConsumer;
import java.time.Duration;
import java.util.Collections;
import java.util.Properties;

public class MinimalConsumerConfig {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Properties props = new Properties();
        // Required: where to find the cluster
        props.put(ConsumerConfig.BOOTSTRAP_SERVERS_CONFIG, "broker1:9092");
        // Required: which group this consumer belongs to
        props.put(ConsumerConfig.GROUP_ID_CONFIG, "settlement-service");
        // Required: how to deserialize key and value
        props.put(ConsumerConfig.KEY_DESERIALIZER_CLASS_CONFIG,
            "org.apache.kafka.common.serialization.StringDeserializer");
        props.put(ConsumerConfig.VALUE_DESERIALIZER_CLASS_CONFIG,
            "org.apache.kafka.common.serialization.StringDeserializer");
        // Recommended: where to start if no committed offset
        props.put(ConsumerConfig.AUTO_OFFSET_RESET_CONFIG, "earliest");
        // Recommended: manual commit for at-least-once
        props.put(ConsumerConfig.ENABLE_AUTO_COMMIT_CONFIG, "false");

        try (KafkaConsumer<String, String> consumer = new KafkaConsumer<>(props)) {
            consumer.subscribe(Collections.singletonList("transfers"));
            ConsumerRecords<String, String> records =
                consumer.poll(Duration.ofSeconds(5));
            records.forEach(r ->
                System.out.println("Received: key=" + r.key() + " value=" + r.value()));
            consumer.commitSync();
        }
    }
}
```

```text
Received: key=SAV2001 value={"amount":50000,"type":"DEBIT"}
```

### How do the parameters relate? (summary table)

| Layer | Parameter | Purpose | Banking value |
|---|---|---|---|
| Producer | bootstrap.servers | Cluster discovery | List 2+ brokers |
| Producer | key/value.serializer | Object to bytes | StringSerializer for JSON |
| Producer | acks | Durability | all |
| Producer | enable.idempotence | No retry duplicates | true |
| Broker | partitions | Parallelism | 6+ |
| Broker | replication.factor | Durability | 3 |
| Broker | min.insync.replicas | acks=all safety | 2 |
| Consumer | bootstrap.servers | Cluster discovery | Same as producer |
| Consumer | group.id | Load sharing | settlement-service |
| Consumer | key/value.deserializer | Bytes to object | Must match producer |
| Consumer | auto.offset.reset | New group start | earliest |
| Consumer | enable.auto.commit | Commit control | false |

### What are common mistakes?

```java
package com.bank.mistake;

public class ConfigMistakes {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // WRONG: serializer/deserializer mismatch
        System.out.println("WRONG: producer=StringSerializer, consumer=AvroDeserializer");
        System.out.println("  Result: garbage or deserialization crash");
        System.out.println();

        // WRONG: single bootstrap server
        System.out.println("WRONG: bootstrap.servers=broker1:9092 (only one)");
        System.out.println("  If broker1 is down, client cannot bootstrap");
        System.out.println();

        // CORRECT
        System.out.println("CORRECT: bootstrap.servers=broker1:9092,broker2:9092");
        System.out.println("  Survives one listed broker being down");
    }
}
```

```text
WRONG: producer=StringSerializer, consumer=AvroDeserializer
  Result: garbage or deserialization crash

WRONG: bootstrap.servers=broker1:9092 (only one)
  If broker1 is down, client cannot bootstrap

CORRECT: bootstrap.servers=broker1:9092,broker2:9092
  Survives one listed broker being down
```

### How does the message key affect flow?

The key determines which partition a record goes to. In banking, key by account id for per-account ordering.

```java
package com.bank.producer;

import org.apache.kafka.clients.producer.ProducerRecord;

public class KeyUsage {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Key = account id -> all events for SAV2001 on same partition
        ProducerRecord<String, String> record = new ProducerRecord<>(
            "transfers", "SAV2001", "{\"amount\":50000}");
        System.out.println("Key: " + record.key());
        System.out.println("All SAV2001 events go to the same partition");
        System.out.println("Ordering guaranteed for this account");
    }
}
```

```text
Key: SAV2001
All SAV2001 events go to the same partition
Ordering guaranteed for this account
```

### What are the default values for key parameters?

Knowing the defaults helps you understand what happens when a parameter is not explicitly set. Many production issues stem from relying on defaults that are optimized for safety or throughput but not for the specific use case.

| Parameter | Default | Notes |
|---|---|---|
| acks | all (Kafka 3.0+) | Was 1 before 3.0 |
| retries | MAX_INT (Kafka 2.1+) | Was 0 before 2.1 |
| enable.idempotence | true (Kafka 3.0+) | Was false before 3.0 |
| max.in.flight.requests | 5 | Must be <=5 for idempotence |
| enable.auto.commit | true | Dangerous for at-least-once |
| auto.offset.reset | latest | Skips backlog for new groups |
| max.poll.records | 500 | Per-poll batch size |
| replication.factor | 1 | Must be >=3 for production |

```java
package com.bank.kafka;

public class DefaultValues {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Key defaults that catch people:");
        System.out.println("  acks=all (safe since Kafka 3.0, was 1 before)");
        System.out.println("  enable.auto.commit=true (dangerous for at-least-once)");
        System.out.println("  auto.offset.reset=latest (skips backlog)");
        System.out.println("  replication.factor=1 (no redundancy)");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Key defaults that catch people:
  acks=all (safe since Kafka 3.0, was 1 before)
  enable.auto.commit=true (dangerous for at-least-once)
  auto.offset.reset=latest (skips backlog)
  replication.factor=1 (no redundancy)
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

The serializer on the producer and the deserializer on the consumer must agree. bootstrap.servers should list at least two brokers. The partition count is a capacity decision made early. Manual commit with auto-commit off gives at-least-once semantics. acks=all plus min.insync.replicas gives durability.
